  "summary_md": "The furnishings sector continues to grapple with soft housing demand and cautious consumer spending, yet a clear operational thread runs through the batch: management teams are extracting margin gains through cost discipline, pricing, and mix improvements — notably at AMWD, COOK, HOFT, and LOVE. Tariff uncertainty and uneven order patterns linger, making bottom-up execution the primary differentiator.\n\nThe most distinctive stories sit at the extremes. SNBR faces an imminent Chapter 11 filing, overwhelming any product-level green shoots and leaving equity holders sidelined. At the same time, a cluster of companies is pursuing radical strategic pivots away from traditional furnishings: ATER is completely exiting consumer e-commerce for African mineral trading and AI-driven exploration, EFOI has won transformative data-center and energy-storage contracts that reshaped its narrative and stock, and XWIN is repositioning as an AI platform play with a SpaceX-linked catalyst. MWYN’s speculative leap into liquid cooling underscores a broader, high-risk wave of non-traditional diversification separating this batch from the industry’s cyclical core.",
